* Dynamic dependency in %cd mode
%cd a <RULE> %cd -
%cd a %cd - <RULE>
* Copy rule in cd mode
* %set vvv aaa %cd ddd <RULE that uses $(vvv)> # Value of $(vvv) is not affected
* What should $(PWD) return?:  Probably the "current" directy based on previous %cd directives
%cd a %cd b %cd - %cd c # Same as %cd a/c
%cd '-' # Directory named '-'
%cd "-" # Directory named '-'
%cd \- # Directory named '-'
%cd $(VAR) # When $VAR is '-':  Directory named '-'
A=B;%cd DIR <RULE> # No whitespace before %%
%cd .
%cd ..
%cd aaa//bbb # double slash is preserved (or not)
%cd //bin # double slash is preserved (or not)
%cd aaa///bbb # Triple slash is preserved (or not)
%cd aaa// # Same as aaa
%cd aaa///// # Same as aaa
%cd // # Stays as-is
%cd /// # Stays as-is (or not)
%cd ////// # Stays as-is (or not)
* Multitarget rule in %cd mode
A = list.dir/B;  list.dir/C {...}  %cd list.dir  B=[C] {...}
A = [list.dir/C] {...}  %cd list.dir  C {...}
* Output redirection in %cd mode
* Input redirection in %cd mode
A: ddd/B;  %cd ddd  B { mkdir . ; }  # Does not work; cd to the directory fails
A: ddd ddd/B;  -p ddd { touch ddd; }  %cd ddd B { echo bbb >B ; }  # Works
* add to https://codeberg.org/brib/slopfree-software-index (Need to add statement to README or doc/)
A: list.dir list.dir/A { cp list.dir/A A ; } -p list.dir {mkdir -p list.dir;} %cd list.dir A {echo CORRECT >A; } # Two rules with target 'A' in different directories
A: list.dir data.a list.dir/data.b {...}  $dir/data.$x {...}  -p list.dir {...}  %cd list.dir  data.b {...}  # The parametrized rule is used for both data.a and data.b
* Concatenation in a %cd rule
A: list.dir [VAR=list.dir/V] {...}  -p list.dir {...}  %cd list.dir  V {...}
A: list.dir list.dir/B {...}  -p list.dir {...}  %cd list.dir  B: $[V] {...}  V {...}
* A dependency that starts with slash is used in a %cd rule: The base directory must not be appended
...  %cd list.dir  V {...} B: $[V] {...}  # Set the variable $V in the command, from file list.dir/V
* Trace of a filename in a %cd rule: target, dependency, dynamic dep, copy dep.
%cd list.dir  ${dir}/file # Do we prepend the base dir to ${dir}? -> maybe we need a new flag to prevent that.  Or automatically recognize that a name starts with ""${param}"/"
* Compound dep used in a %cd rule
* Concatenation used in a %cd rule
* main.stu does %cd immediately and then a rule.  The default rule is %cd rule.
* In %cd rule:  dependency /aaa[B] is not rebased because it is absolute
* Canonicalization:  A: list.dir/x/y;  %cd list.dir//x  y {...}  # The rule for 'y' must match the dependency.
* Canonicalization:  A: list.dir//x/y;  %cd list.dir/./x  y {...}  # The rule for 'y' must match the dependency.
%cd ./
%cd dir/.
%cd dir/./
%cd dir/./.
%cd ./dir
%cd list.dir A: nonexistingfile # The error message lists 'list.dir/nonexistingfile' as not found, not 'nonexistingfile'
* -P option with %cd rules
%cd      - # With many spaces
%cd   \<newline>   - # With actual newline
* "%cd -" inside dynamic dependency
%cd- # Does this work?
%cd -X # Error, not just '%cd -' followed by 'X' (keep syntax for future extension)
* 10 times "%cd aaa" followed by 10 times "%cd -"
$ stu -F '%cd DIR' -f aaa.stu # rules in aaa.stu are interpreted with base dir DIR
$ stu -F '%cd' # Error
%cd $(VAR) # when $VAR starts with a dash
* The default rule is a %cd rule.  I.e., a %cd followed by the first rule.
* A %cd rule contains a dependency or target '../file', which is also referenced in the non-cd part
* Multiply nested '%include -d'-like %cd+%include combinations
* Syntax error in a file included during %cd:  The trace into the file includes the '%cd' directory
$ stu -C '%cd DIR' # Error
* File that ends in a valid '%cd DIR'
* File that ends in a valid '%cd -'

#
# %include -d
#

# Import all rules from subdirectory; will prepend the directory name to
# all targets.  Rules from there will be executed from within the
# directory as current directory.  If using 'import', the meaning of
# all these are equivalent:
%include -d src
%include --directory src/main.stu

# Without cd-ing, i.e. "library mode".  Allows paths with slashes, but also
# $STU_{,LIB_}PATH.
%include --library c++.stu
%include -l c++/simple.stu
%include -l /usr/stulib/c++.stu
* Need to determine in which cases we use $STU_LIB_PATH and in which not.

* Need to determine whether ".stu" is automatically appended to names (No)
* Need to determine whether we want different filenames for "normal" Stu
  files (applying to the current directory) and Stu
  libraries (that should only be imported via '%import -l' (e.g. *.stl).  That also means
  that '%include' will by default apply to Stu libraries, and that is not good.

# - What about phony targets imported in that way.  Do we
#   prepend a directory to them?
%import dir   # contains:   @all:  ...;
# gets converted to:       @dir/all:  ...;
# or to:                   dir/@all:  ...;  # ???

# File import should also work in the command line
$ stu -d dir/main.stu # Like first cd'ing to dir/

# Can we even use '%', because '%' directives are token-based, and 'import' should be at
# rule level.  Maybe use '*' instead.  (No).
*import dir/
*import -d dir/

# And theoretically also braces for blocks

# * Check that $(PWD) is always the base dir of the rule

#
# When doing %include or %import, allow to pass environment variables.  (Discuss.)
#

#
# Have an option/flag for: For content rules, compare the content of the file with
# the wanted content and don't update the file if identical.  Very useful when someone
# changes the content in the stu file, and then re-builds.  At the moment, it is a common
# complaint that Stu does _not_ re-build in that case.
#
# This should be a flag before the content rule.
#
# Check the date of the Stu file before looking at the content.  Maybe do that by default.
#

# When this is changed in the Stu file, everything that uses CFLAGS is rebuilt.
CFLAGS = -X { -O2 }

# Or:
#
# For content rules, maybe have an own Executor subclass that checks whether the existing
# has the same content as the to-be-written content, and if it has, does not touch the old
# file.  This would make it possible that things are re-built when the content of {...} in
# the Stu file is changed.
#
# This behavior could also be implemented in File_Executor, by querying hashes of each
# file's content and timestamp before execution, and if after execution the content has
# not change, revert the timestamps to their old values.  That would be expensive though
# due to scanning a whole file, and should be disabled by default.

#
# Line number and file syntax:  A preprocessor-like construct that gives a new
# filename and line number, such that error messages from Stu can point
# to the original file, not the generated file.  Analogous to the
# preprocessors #line directive.  Useful in dynamic dependencies.
#

% file datasets.list
% line 120

# Use this in stu-utils/texdep and other stu-utils programs judiciously.

#
# When including files using dynamic dependencies, make a
# difference between inclusion and import, as is done for %include and
# %import.
#

A: [-d data/B];

#
# In -j>1 mode, Start trivial dependencies immediately when it is clear
# they must be started, not just when everything else is done.
#

* Maybe this is already the case?

#
# Search within predefined paths for %include ($STU_PATH or $STUPATH;
# the -I option)
#

# This is included from the "Stu path", and could be installed in
# e.g. /usr/share/stu/lib/ or ~/share/stu/lib/, etc.
% include c++.stu

#
# A flag with the meaning "never rebuild that dependency", but only use
# it to determine whether we need to rebuilt the target.  It would mean
# that we can prune dependencies with that flag as soon as we know that
# the target must be built anyway.
#

# In this example, when 'program' does not exist, Stu will *not* build
# 'dep.sources', because the -s flag is used and Stu knows that
# 'program' has to be rebuilt anyway.  '-s' could stand for
# "secondary", and mean "we don't want this to be built, we only need it
# to determine whether we have to be rebuilt."

program:  -s [dep.sources] {
	cc ... -o program
}
dep.sources {
	# Some complex mechanism for determining the source code files
	# of the program
}

# This may be identical to
program:  [-o -p dep.sources] { ... }
# and "dep.sources" may even be a side effect of "program".

# This could be the first case where "-p -o" makes sense.  We may want to allow "-po" for
# this.

#
# Regular expressions in target names.
#
# * How to handle the case with multiple targets.
#

list.${X:.}${Y:.} {
	compute -x $X -y $Y >list.${X}${Y}
}

# When the date is saved as contiguous digits, e.g. 'log-analysis.20180413.txt'
# for April 13, 2018, but as ISO dat in 'log-*.txt'.
log-analysis.${year:[0-9]{4}}${month:[0-9]{2}}${day:[0-9]{2}}.txt:
	log.$year-$month-$dat.txt
{ ... }

# The parameter $NETWORK must not contain a dot
web.${NETWORK:[^.]+} :  ... ;

# Specify a list of values
${NAME:ref|ukob|tuberlin}.bib:  bibs-A/${NAME}.bib
{ cp bibs-A/$NAME.bib $NAME.bib }
$NAME.bib:  bibs-B/$NAME.bib
{ cp bibs-B/$NAME.bib $NAME.bib }

# Exclude slashes
${NAME:[^/]+}.bib: ...;

# Can also be done with a pipe:
list.${name|[A-Z].*} # Matches uppercase names
# Find a good syntax based on Bash/KSH.

#
# Priority levels for rules.
#

# The second rule matches because the priority is higher.  Without the
# priorities, it would be an error because no rule dominates the other.
$x.bbb -p1:  ... { ... }
aaa.$x -p2:  ... { ... }

